// line for line (same knots, lines, speakers and expressions; the parity test in Story/Tests checks it). Change the
// dialect file first, then this one. [[word]] marks a tap-to-explain word; its English meaning is in the Glossary table.

// The saved choice for a knot ("" before it's made), bound by the game (STORY_BIBLE.md section 8). No
// regular scene reads one yet.
EXTERNAL chose(knot)

=== abu_fadi_3 ===
You know, Salma… I had someone I used to wait for here, too. #abu_fadi
Every Friday she sat by the window, ordered her coffee without sugar, and didn't say a word. #abu_fadi
And what happened, Uncle? #salma
She left in '76. And I kept coming every Friday, in case she came back. Your Teta knew, and never told a soul. #abu_fadi
-> END

=== abu_fadi_6 ===
Come and sit, Salma. I want to show you something I've never shown anyone. #abu_fadi
From his pocket, Abu Fadi takes a small black-and-white photo: a young woman sitting by this very window, her eyes on the street. #sys
Oh, she's beautiful… Is that her, Uncle? What was her name? #salma #surprised
The photo is enough for today, my girl. The name… let's leave it for another day. #abu_fadi #sad
-> END

=== abu_fadi_10 ===
Her name is Najwa. Fifty years, and I've never said it out loud. #abu_fadi
Najwa… And Teta knew? #salma #surprised
She knew her name, and never once said it in front of me. That was her kindness: she kept quiet so it wouldn't hurt me. #abu_fadi
Take this clock, my girl, the window clock. Fifty years I counted the Friday minutes on it… but now I don't need to count any more. #abu_fadi #smile
-> END

=== tala_3 ===
Can I study here? At home my four brothers never stop shouting, and I can't concentrate. #tala
Of course! What are you studying? #salma
It's my [[Tawjihi]] year, and the exams are in three months. And I still haven't finished chemistry. #tala #sad
Then the quiet corner is yours. And every chapter you finish, a [[ma'amoul]] on the house. #salma #smile
-> END

=== tala_6 ===
Tala sits in the corner with an exam paper in front of her. There's a big red mark on it. #sys
I failed the mock exam. That's it, I'm not cut out for this. I'm quitting school. #tala #sad
So what? I failed maths once. Now I'm the one who keeps the café's books. #lana
A mock exam shows you where you went wrong. It's not a reason to quit. Come on, from question one. #salma
-> END

=== tala_10 ===
The results are out! I passed, and my grades get me into pharmacy! #tala #smile
Congratulations, Tala! If Teta were here, she'd have handed out ma'amoul to the whole street. #salma #smile
Take the study lamp I stayed up under all year. Keep it in the corner, for whoever comes to study here after me. #tala
-> END

=== um_rami_3 ===
Good morning, Salma. May I leave my cards by the till? #um_rami
Cards? What do you do, Um Rami? #salma
I cook from home. Since Abu Rami passed, God rest him, cooking is what keeps Rami and me going. #um_rami #sad
Put them at the front, by the dallah. And the first order is mine. #salma #smile
-> END

=== um_rami_6 ===
Fifty plates of [[maqluba]], the big pot is burned, and the dinner is in two hours! #um_rami #sad
Don't worry! I'll chop, Salma will cook, and you just tell us what to do. #lana
Two hours is plenty. Teta could cook for a whole wedding in two hours. Let's go! #salma
Two hours later, right on time, the trays went out of the door, and their smell filled the street. #sys
I'll never forget this. Not me, and not Rami. #um_rami #smile
-> END

=== um_rami_10 ===
Salma! I got the biggest order of my life: Dr. Hiba's wedding. Two hundred plates! #um_rami #surprised
Two hundred?! Um Rami, that's not a home kitchen, that's a restaurant! #salma #surprised
Remember the day of the burned pot? Without you, I'd have thrown my cards away and stayed home. #um_rami
Take this painted clay plate, the first thing I bought with my cooking money. Hang it on the wall, so we remember we started together. #um_rami #smile
-> END

=== karim_3 ===
My master built this counter, God rest him, in '74. Over fifty years, and it's never once complained. #karim
So you've known Teta all this time, Uncle Karim? #salma #surprised
Since I was a boy in his workshop. Whenever the [[Hajjeh]] needed something, he sent me. #karim
She used to say: wood is like coffee, it needs patience. And my master would laugh, because those were his words. #karim #smile
-> END

=== karim_6 ===
Uncle Karim, why did you never marry? #lana
I married the workshop. Five saws and three hammers, and every one of them does as it's told. #karim #smile
And every door I've made in this neighbourhood is one of my children. I walk past and say hello. #karim
So half the street is your family? #salma #smile
And the counter's the eldest. And you two… you're family now as well. #karim
-> END

=== karim_10 ===
Lana, every day you tell me Riyadh has no café like this one. #karim
And I'll say it again! Why? #lana
Uncle Karim, what are you hiding behind your back? #salma #surprised
A little olive-wood sign: “[[Gahwit Sitti]] · Riyadh”. Hang it here until its day comes. Wood knows before people do. #karim #smile
-> END

=== samira_3 ===
My husband's in Dubai, Salma. You must have seen him there! Everyone knows him. #samira
[[Khalto]], Dubai has three million people. #salma
And they all know him! A manager, sweetheart, a manager. Every Eid he sends me a gold bracelet. #samira
This year it's a little late… The bracelet must be too big to fit in the envelope. #samira #smile
-> END

=== samira_6 ===
Salma, do you want the truth? My husband hasn't come home in two years. Not once. #samira #sad
Two years, Khalto? And you talk about him every day… #salma
I talk about him so I don't forget him. Or so he doesn't forget me… I don't know which. #samira
But not a word to anyone! On my children's lives, if this gets out, I'll bring you a groom every Thursday. #samira #smile
-> END

=== samira_10 ===
Khalto Samira comes in without a word. Behind her is a white-haired man carrying a suitcase. #sys
This is… my husband. He came for Dr. Hiba's wedding. He said: a wedding you arranged, I won't miss. #samira
Welcome, Uncle! Khalto, why so quiet? I've never seen you like this. #salma #smile
Let me be quiet for a bit, Salma. Just a bit. #samira #smile
Take this embroidered cushion. I stitched it on the nights I waited for him, and now I have someone to sit beside me. #samira #smile
-> END

=== dr_hiba_3 ===
Two in the morning, and you're still open? God bless you. #dr_hiba #surprised
Who would I close for? You stop by every night after the hospital. #salma
Night shift, every day. Your coffee is the only warm thing in the whole night. #dr_hiba
Then the light stays on. For you, and for everyone who works while the city sleeps. #salma #smile
-> END

=== dr_hiba_6 ===
Two in the morning. At the table next to Dr. Hiba sits the pharmacist Khalto Samira brought. #sys
Salma, who is that? He knows every medicine I write, and he can even read my handwriting! #dr_hiba #surprised
That's Hani, the night pharmacist from the corner. Khalto brought him for me… but it looks like he came for you. #salma #smile
If his intentions are good, he knows where my family lives. Until then… not a word to your Khalto Samira. #dr_hiba #smile
-> END

=== dr_hiba_10 ===
Hani came to our house with his family, and my father said yes. We read the [[Fatiha]] on Friday! #dr_hiba #smile
Congratulations, Doctor! That news is sweeter than coffee. #salma #surprised
And I want you and Lana to do the coffee and sweets for the wedding. Everything. #dr_hiba
Consider it done! But which do you want: Ammani coffee or Saudi? #lana #smile
Both, like you two. And take this night jasmine from my mother's garden: it opens at night, like me, and like your café. #dr_hiba #smile
-> END

=== yazan_3 ===
Outside on the pavement, Yazan plays the [[oud]], with a tin in front of him holding a few coins. #sys
Yazan! Why are you always out in the cold? Come inside and play for us. #salma
Me? But I can't even pay for a coffee. #yazan #surprised
The music pays for it. Teta used to say: a café without songs is like coffee without cardamom. #salma #smile
Anything for you. But careful, I'll play until you throw me out. #yazan #smile
-> END

=== yazan_6 ===
Yazan tunes the oud, then plays an old, slow melody. #sys
Wait… that song! Teta sang it every Friday with the radio. #salma #surprised
My grandfather taught me. He used to say that in old Jabal Amman, every window sang it. #yazan
Even my mom hums it in the kitchen… and she's never told me where it's from. #lana #thinking
Then it's the café's song now. Whenever you ask, I'll play it. #yazan #smile
-> END

=== yazan_10 ===
Salma! Dr. Hiba's wedding… they've asked me to play. My first wedding, and a paid one! #yazan #smile
From the pavement to a wedding? Yazan, you're a real musician now! #salma #smile
From the pavement to here, and from here to the wedding. All of it through your café's door. #yazan
Take my old oud, the one I played outside in the cold. Hang it on the wall. Maybe someone else will find himself in it. #yazan
-> END

=== sara_noor_3 ===
Sara and Noor, newlyweds, sit at one table doing sums on a napkin. #sys
Sara: If we don't buy the sofa now, we'll be sitting on the floor till the end of the month. #sara_noor
Noor: What's wrong with the floor? My grandmother sat on the floor for sixty years and lived happily. #sara_noor
Two coffees on the house. And when you've finished the sums, tell me who won. #salma #smile
-> END

=== sara_noor_6 ===
Sara: We have news… but we haven't told anyone yet. #sara_noor #smile
Noor: Meaning we've told half the street. But you're the first at the café. #sara_noor
No! Congratulations! A boy or a girl? #salma #surprised
Sara: A girl. And Noor's already saving for her university. #sara_noor #smile
Finally, a fight about money that's worth having. #lana #smile
-> END

=== sara_noor_10 ===
Sara: We've decided. Her first outing from home will be here. #sara_noor #smile
Noor: And we'll sit her at the same table where we used to argue about money. #sara_noor
Then I'm reserving the table for her from today. What will you call her? #salma #smile
Sara: We still haven't agreed… but we did agree on this little wooden chair. Keep it here, Salma, waiting for her. #sara_noor
-> END

=== postman_3 ===
My girl, I owe you an apology… and I don't know where to begin. #postman
An apology? For what, Uncle? #salma #surprised
Years ago, there was a letter for this café, and I never delivered it. #postman #sad
It's sat on my heart like a stone. Give me time, my girl… today I just want a coffee. #postman
-> END

=== postman_6 ===
The letter I told you about… it was for your Teta. From Riyadh, in 2007. #postman
From Riyadh? In 2007? #salma #thinking
On the envelope, the sender's name: Nawal. #postman
Aunt Nawal… a year after the falling-out. #salma #surprised
I found it behind the sorting cabinet when they closed the old office. I came to the door three times, and couldn't knock. #postman #sad
-> END

=== postman_10 ===
Lana, this is yours now. It should have arrived long ago… forgive me. #postman
Lana opens the envelope. Her mother's handwriting, and the date: Riyadh, 2007. #sys
“Mama, forgive me for leaving without saying goodbye. A promise is still a promise… but tell Rana I miss her.” #lana #sad
Mom wrote to her, and Grandma never got it. Neither of them ever knew. #lana #sad
And Salma, here's a wooden letterbox for the door. From today, no letter for this café gets lost. #postman
-> END

=== mrs_odeh_3 ===
You're Rana's daughter? I'd know those eyes anywhere. I taught your mother Arabic, in ninth grade. #mrs_odeh #smile
Mrs. Odeh? Mom remembers you so well. #salma #surprised
How is Rana? And why doesn't she come? This is her mother's café! #mrs_odeh
Mom… hasn't set foot in the café for a long time. #salma #sad
Give her my love, and tell her: Mrs. Odeh is still waiting for her homework. #mrs_odeh #smile
-> END

=== mrs_odeh_6 ===
Your mother was my brightest pupil… and my angriest, too. #mrs_odeh
Mom? Angry? When Mom's upset, she goes quiet. #salma #surprised
As a girl she'd raise her voice, and cry if anyone was unfair to her friend. The silence she learned later. #mrs_odeh
My mom too… she goes quiet exactly like that. #lana #thinking
Someone who goes that quiet, girls, isn't angry. She's hurting. #mrs_odeh #sad
-> END

=== mrs_odeh_10 ===
Mrs. Odeh takes out her phone and dials a number she knows by heart. #sys
Rana? It's Mrs. Odeh. There's a wedding in your mother's street, and I want you sitting next to me. #mrs_odeh
I'm not asking you to come into the café, dear. The wedding's in the street. I'm asking you to sit beside your teacher. #mrs_odeh
She said: “We'll see.” And from Rana, “we'll see” means yes. #mrs_odeh #smile
And this is for you, Salma: my brass school bell. I rang it for thirty years. Hang it by the door… maybe one day it will ring for Rana. #mrs_odeh
-> END

=== faris_3 ===
Your cardamom's a touch above standard. A bold choice… I respect the concept. #faris
What are you, a health inspector? #salma #surprised
No, no. Just a regular customer. I like coffee… a lot. #faris #smile
But in the old days, coffee here came with a glass of water before it, not after. #faris
…And how would you know what it was like in the old days? #salma #thinking
-> END

=== faris_6 ===
I told you I came here with my father every Friday. I didn't tell you what I ate. #faris
Your Teta gave me the corner piece of knafeh, the crispy one, and said: this one's only for clever boys. #faris #smile
And then it turned up in your branches, right? #lana
It did. But it never tasted the same. I told my father so when I was sixteen, and he went quiet… the way he still does. #faris #sad
Maybe the taste was never in the recipe, Faris. It was in the hand that gave you the piece. #salma
-> END

=== faris_10 ===
I know now what my father's deal cost your Teta. Thirteen years of paying, one cup at a time. #faris #sad
What are you trying to say, Faris? #lana
That I can't give back a single coin. But if you ever open in Riyadh, the first sack of beans is from me, and the rest at cost. #faris
Is that business, or an apology? #salma #thinking
An apology. And take this, my father's first grinder, from the first little shop he opened. It belongs here, not with us. #faris #smile
-> END

=== tourist_3 ===
May I sit by the window? And a coffee without sugar, please. #tourist
Of course. You're the first guest from Jeddah who's asked for no sugar. #salma #smile
I lived in Amman long ago, as a young woman. Fifty years in Jeddah, and I never forgot the taste of its coffee. #tourist #smile
Your coffee is very good… just as it was. #tourist #thinking
-> END

=== tourist_6 ===
My girl, there used to be a man who sat here every Friday, facing the window… Does he still come? #tourist
Uncle Abu Fadi? He comes every day! That's his table, reserved for him. #salma
Every day…? Don't tell him I asked. Not yet. #tourist #sad
Salma, what's going on? #lana #thinking
I don't know… but now I have a question for Uncle. #salma #thinking
-> END

=== tourist_10 ===
Tomorrow I fly back to Jeddah. But before I go… this letter is for Abu Fadi. #tourist
Why not give it to him yourself? He'll be here any minute! #salma #surprised
Because if I see him, I won't be able to leave. Give it to him after I've gone. #tourist #sad
And this is for you, my girl: a little wooden [[roshan]] from the old houses of Jeddah. Put it by his window. #tourist #smile
-> END
